  component: component:AgentsPanel
```

## Features

```mdl
feat F-1: Agents

  req F-1.1: The space's AI group lists every Agent in an Agents section; + creates an autonomous agent.
  req F-1.2:
    when: op:createAgent runs
    then: an Agent exists with Instructions holding the Interview skill and a first chat bound to the Agent conversation skill
  req F-1.3: op:listAgents reports each Agent with the number of chats parented to it.
  req F-1.4: A space with no agent creates its first from "Add to space" › Agent, named in the form.
```

```mdl
feat F-2: Channel conversations

  req F-2.1:
    when: op:ensureChannelChat is called twice for the same agent, channel and thread
    then: the same chat and feed are returned
  req F-2.2:
    when: a new conversation arrives
    then: a new chat is parented to the agent, named after the conversation, sharing the agent's Instructions, keyed by channel and thread
  req F-2.3: The same conversation bridged to two agents yields two chats.
  req F-2.4: A channel and a thread inside it are separate conversations.
```

```mdl
feat F-3: Agent channels and delivery

  req F-3.1: An Agent's Activity companion shows a channel picker; the first save creates an AgentChannels parented to the agent.
  req F-3.2: The list is deleted with its agent and found from it by AgentChannels.loadForAgent.
  req F-3.3: Under the picker, each channel shows its backend's ObjectProperties surface (plugin-discord: bot form, status, Start/Stop).
  req F-3.4:
    when: op:sendMessage reaches a person with no chat
    then: the first of the agent's channels whose backend opens a direct conversation posts it, and that conversation becomes the person's chat
  req F-3.5:
    when: a relay's requester is reported to
    then: the report goes to the relay's replyChannel and replyThread first
  req F-3.6:
    when: the backend refuses, or no channel can reach the person
    then: op:sendMessage returns delivered false with a reason the agent can relay, and records nothing
  req F-3.7: The Activity companion lists the agent's channel chats newest first, with title and last activity; selecting one opens the chat.
```

```mdl
feat F-4: Memory graph

  req F-4.1: op:resolveEntity matches a Person by handle first, then case-insensitively by name, and creates one only when nothing matches.
  req F-4.2: op:recordMemory links the memory to every subject by HasSubject and rejects an empty subject list.
  req F-4.3:
    when: a memory is recorded with supersedes
    then: the old memory becomes superseded and is no longer recalled
  req F-4.4: op:retrieveMemories returns active memories only, filtered by subject and query, and the subject's live goals.
  req F-4.5: op:suggestGoal creates a proposed goal and rejects owners that are not people or organizations.
  req F-4.6: op:setGoalStatus confirms a goal by default.
  req F-4.7: op:updateProfile keeps one profile document per subject and rewrites it in place.
```

```mdl
feat F-5: Interview and profile panel

  req F-5.1: An agent with the Interview skill resolves the interviewee, records memories and proposes goals as the conversation runs, and confirms goals at the end.
  req F-5.2: A Person's or Organization's properties list its live goals and active memories.
  req F-5.3:
    when: a goal's status or a memory's status changes
    then: the properties panel updates without reopening
```

```mdl
feat F-6: An agent in several spaces

  req F-6.1: A space's Members panel offers, in its "Add from contacts" picker after the contacts, the agents the member administers that are not in the space yet, including those whose home space the member cannot reach; Add brings the agent into the space. An agent's identity, a contact to everyone in the agent's own space, is not offered as a contact (`AppSpace.agentIdentityKeys`), so the picker lists each agent once.
  req F-6.2:
    when: op:inviteAgent runs for an agent already in the space (its home, or an earlier presence)
    then: that agent is returned and nothing is created
  req F-6.3: A presence carries its home's name, DID and instructions, keyed to the home agent's URI; inviting a presence keys the new one to the same home. An admin who cannot reach the home invites from the agent's own space instead (F-7.5), and the presence starts from the base instructions for its name.
  req F-6.4:
    when: a member opens an agent invited from another space
    then: its article is the member's one private chat with the agent, the same as at home, and its companions show the home agent's knowledge and activity, when the member can reach the home space; otherwise the presence's own
  req F-6.5: A presence keys its facts to its home's brain (`AgentPresence.brainId`): the end-of-turn read adds to it and op:retrieveMemories reads it. Triggers stay per agent object. EDGE runs the plugin-agent its catalog pins, so its turns do this once edge takes this version.
  req F-6.6: Inviting writes no conversation: nothing is quoted from any chat into the space.
  req F-6.7: Only an agent's admins can add it to another space. The picker offers only the agents the member administers, and op:inviteAgent registers the space with EDGE first, which refuses anyone else; EDGE's brain serves only its home and the spaces registered so, so a presence made any other way reads and writes nothing of the brain, and EDGE runs no turn of its chats.
```

```mdl
feat F-7: An agent's own space

  req F-7.1: An agent with an identity has a default space its identity owns, minted with it by op:ensureIdentity and tagged `AppSpace.AGENT_SPACE_TAG`; the identity is its owner, named after the agent, and every other current member is one of the agent's admins.
  req F-7.2: `AgentSpace.administeredBy` lists the agents an identity administers: the agent spaces among the client's own spaces, which its HALO holds memberships of, that the identity is a current member of. It asks nothing of EDGE.
  req F-7.3: `AgentSpace.admins` lists an agent's admins, every current member of its space but the agent, and `AgentSpace.agentOf` is the agent.
  req F-7.4: The account's Agents panel, right after Devices, lists the agents the identity administers (F-7.2): each one's name, its admins with the viewer marked, and Open space, which switches to the agent's space. It stays current as spaces and their members change.
  req F-7.5: EDGE keys an agent space's properties to the agent's URI (`AgentPresence.HOME_SOURCE`) when it mints the space. `AgentSpace.administeredAgents` reads each administered agent's name, DID and home from its space, so an admin who is not a member of the agent's home space can still add the agent to other spaces (F-6.1).
  req F-7.6: An agent with an identity is a member of the shared spaces it is in. op:ensureIdentity admits its identity to the space the agent is created in, and op:inviteAgent to the space it joins, as an editor named after the agent and marked as an agent (`SpaceMember.agent`). A locked space (a personal or private one, `MembershipPolicy.LOCKED`) takes no other members after its genesis, but its owner admits an agent and manages its role, short of admin. Only a space's admins can admit members: the agent is created without the membership for anyone else, and the invitation fails.
  req F-7.7: Creating an agent opens it at once. op:ensureIdentity runs in the background (its identity, its own space, its membership), and the agent's chat takes messages meanwhile: it runs on EDGE without an identity, and a message sent before EDGE has the new chat is delivered once it does.
```

## Acceptance

Derived criteria, each executed by a vitest case or a storybook play function.

```mdl
scenario T-1: Channel chats are idempotent per conversation   # ensure-channel-chat.test.ts
  # covers [F-2.1, F-2.2, F-2.4]
  given: an agent created by op:createAgent and a channel on a test backend
  when: ensureChannelChat for thread-1 (title Hello), again for thread-1, then for thread-2 and for the channel itself
  then:
    - both thread-1 calls return the same chat and feed; thread-2 and the channel each return another
    - the thread chat is not the agent's first chat, is named Hello and is parented to the agent
    - it shares the agent's Instructions; its only meta key is AgentChannels.chatKey(channel, thread-1)
```

```mdl
scenario T-2: Conversations are separate per agent   # ensure-channel-chat.test.ts
  # covers [F-2.3, F-1.3]
  given: agents First and Second and one channel
  when: each ensures a chat for thread "shared"
  then:
    - the two chats differ
    - listAgents reports First and Second with 2 chats each
```

```mdl
scenario T-3: Channels belong to their agent   # ensure-channel-chat.test.ts
  # covers [F-3.2]
  given: an agent and a channel
  when: AgentChannels.make for the agent with the channel is added
  then: its parent is the agent, loadForAgent returns it and loadChannels returns the channel
```

```mdl
scenario T-4: An interview builds the graph   # interview.test.ts
  # covers [F-4.1, F-4.2, F-4.3, F-4.4, F-4.5, F-4.6, F-4.7]
  when: resolve "Rich Burdon" with a discord handle, then by handle, then by "rich burdon"; resolve the
    organization "Composer team"; remember three claims; propose two goals and confirm one; supersede
    one memory; recall; update the profile twice
  then:
    - the first resolve creates a Person; the other two return it
    - the superseded memory is superseded; recall returns the three active claims and both goals (confirmed, proposed)
    - recall with query "STANDUPS" returns only the matching memory; the team recalls only the shared memory and no goals
    - both updateProfile calls return the same document, with exactly one ProfileOf
    - the profile contains the name, the confirmed goal and the new claim, and not the superseded one
```

```mdl
scenario T-5: Invalid memory and goal input is rejected   # interview.test.ts
  # covers [F-4.2, F-4.5]
  then:
    - recordMemory with no subjects fails with "at least one subject"
    - suggestGoal owned by a Memory fails with "people or organizations"
```

```mdl
scenario T-6: Relays travel through channels   # relay.test.ts (test channel backend)
  # covers [F-3.4, F-3.5, F-3.6]
  then:
    - a person with a handle but no chat is reached by a direct thread on the agent's channel, which becomes their chat with the backend's receipt on the recorded message
    - the report to the requester goes to the relay's replyChannel and replyThread
    - a refused direct message returns the backend's reason and records no chat
    - an agent with no channels, or a person with no handle, is not delivered to, with a reason
```

```mdl
scenario T-8: Scripted interview fills the profile panel
  # covers [F-5.1, F-5.2, F-5.3]
  # stories-assistant Interview › TestInterviewScripted (ScriptedLanguageModel, four turns)
  given: the agent's Chat beside the interviewee's profile panel, with a scripted model
  when: Rich introduces himself, states two goals, names the blocker, confirms both goals
  then:
    - the Goals region lists both goals and reads "Confirmed" twice
    - the Memories region lists the three recorded claims
    - the space holds one Person, two confirmed goals and at least three memories
```

```mdl
scenario T-9: An invited agent keeps one brain   # invite-agent.test.ts
  # covers [F-6.1, F-6.2, F-6.3, F-6.5, F-6.6]
  given: Kai in a home space; a fact Mykola stated in Kai's home brain
  when: Kai is invited into a second space twice, then as its presence into a third, then back home; the presence recalls and learns
  then:
    - one presence exists, keyed to Kai's URI, with Kai's name, DID and instructions; back home Kai itself is returned
    - every presence names Kai's brain; the presence's chat is empty
    - op:retrieveMemories in the presence's space returns the home fact, said by Mykola
    - a fact the presence reads lands in the home brain, and the Brain companion lists both
    - the picker offers Kai by its URI only where it is not yet: not in its home, nor its own space, nor where it has a presence; for an agent space minted before EDGE recorded the home, by its DID
    - an admin who cannot reach Kai's home invites it from what Kai's space records: a presence keyed to Kai's URI with its name, its DID and the base instructions; inviting again returns it
```

```mdl
scenario T-10: Agent spaces list their admins   # AgentSpace.test.ts
  # covers [F-7.2, F-7.3, F-7.5]
  given: an agent space owned by the agent, with an admin and an editor; an agent space the identity was removed from; an untagged space
  then:
    - agentOf is the owner, and the admins are the admin and the editor
    - the identity administers only the first space, and another identity none
    - administeredAgents reads the name, DID, identity key and home URI an open agent space records, and only the DID and identity key of one still opening
```

```mdl
scenario T-11: An agent is admitted to a space as a member   # admit-agent.test.ts
  # covers [F-7.6]
  given: a space owned by someone else
  then:
    - the agent's identity is admitted as an editor, named after the agent and marked as one
    - a current member is left as it is, and one that was removed is admitted again
    - a space that refuses the admission fails it with AdmitAgentError
```

## Not built

Design items from `docs/DESIGN.md` and `docs/MEMORY.md` that this package does not implement:

- The EDGE gateway Durable Object and the agent turn on EDGE live outside this package (EDGE repo).
  Until edge step 4 the gateway still reads a `DiscordBinding` and calls `ensureThreadChat`, neither of
  which exists any more, so Discord conversations do not reach the agent.
- The intended shape: an agent is a member of the spaces it is invited to, people talk to it there as a
  bot in their chats, and each person has one conversation with it at the top level (L0), not one per space.
  As built, an agent with an identity is a member of each space it is in (F-7.6), but people talk to it
  through a presence (an `Agent` keyed to its home); the member's one conversation is their private chat in
  the agent's home space, which the presence's article opens; its messages carry no identity (`sender` is
  `{ role: 'assistant' }`); and chats in a space do not wake the agent.
- Removing an agent from a space's members leaves its presence there and the space registered with its brain.
- A member who cannot reach the agent's home space talks to the presence in a chat of its own, which reads
  the home's brain but not the home conversation.
- Memories and goals are objects in the space they were recorded in; only facts are shared through the
  brain.
- Runtime-assembled memory context at the start of each turn; consolidation (dedupe, promotion of
  inferred memories); editing or retracting memories in the UI (`retracted` exists as a status only).
- Required provenance: `Memory.source` is optional as built.
- The interview checklist on `Chat.tasks`: the skill uses a checklist only if a tool for it is bound.

## QA

Executable tests. Each step carries a human instruction (`do`) and, where the step maps to an
invocable operation, an agent binding (`invoke`). Report failures by test and step number.

```mdl
test QA-1: Create an autonomous agent, give it a Discord channel, start the bot
  desc: |
    The primary journey: an agent from the Agents section, a Discord-backed channel picked as one of
    its channels, and the bot control round-trip to EDGE through plugin-thread's generic channel
    operations. Run against a local EDGE with a deliberately invalid bot token, so the gateway cannot
    connect and the status must carry the gateway's error — a "Connected" banner here is a defect.
  actors: human
  covers: [F-1.1, F-3.1, F-3.3]
  status: unverified         # never run against the app
  given:
    - an identity exists
    - space: a space is open and SPACE_READY
    - plugin-agent and plugin-discord are enabled (both are tagged labs) and Composer points at a local EDGE
    - no Agent named "QA: Interlocutor $runId" exists
  before:
    - name: Add a bot token
      do: Add an access token with source "discord.com", account "QA: bot $runId" and value "qa-invalid-token".
      invoke: org.dxos.operation.space.addObject { object: { "@type": "org.dxos.type.accessToken", source: "discord.com", account: "QA: bot $runId", token: "qa-invalid-token" } }
      capture: token
      expect: the token exists in the space
    - name: Create a Discord channel
      do: |
        Create a channel named "QA: discord $runId" with the Discord backend: Bot token "QA: bot $runId",
        Application ID "qa-app-$runId", channel "qa-channel-$runId".
      invoke: org.dxos.operation.thread.createChannel { spaceId: $given.space.id, name: "QA: discord $runId", kind: "org.dxos.channel.backend.discord", options: { accessToken: Ref($token.object), applicationId: "qa-app-$runId", channels: ["qa-channel-$runId"] } }
      capture: channel
      expect: the channel exists and its properties show the Discord form and a bot status banner
  steps:
    - name: Create the agent
      do: |
        In the navtree, open the space's AI group, click + on the Agents section ("Add agent"), and
        rename the new "New agent" to "QA: Interlocutor $runId".
      invoke: [op:createAgent] { name: "QA: Interlocutor $runId" }
      capture: agent
      expect: the agent appears under Agents with that name
    - name: Give the agent the channel
      do: Open the agent's Activity companion and pick "QA: discord $runId" under Channels.
      expect: |
        the Discord form, the bot status banner and the Start/Stop/Refresh toolbar appear under the
        picker, and "Conversations" reads "No channel conversations yet."
      note: NO `invoke` — the container saves AgentChannels with `db.add`. This step is why the test is `actors: human`.
    - name: Start the bot
      do: Click Start bot.
      invoke: org.dxos.operation.thread.connectChannel { channel: Ref($channel.object) }
      expect: the toolbar is briefly disabled, then the banner shows a gateway state other than Connected
      assert: return $result.status.state !== 'ready' && $stepErrors.length === 0
      note: An invocation failure (EDGE not running) means the fixture is wrong — report the step as blocked.
    - name: Read the status
      do: Click Refresh status.
      invoke: org.dxos.operation.thread.getChannelStatus { channel: Ref($channel.object) }
      expect: the banner reads "Failed" (or "Connecting" while EDGE retries) and shows the gateway's error; never "Connected"
      assert: return $result.status.state !== 'ready' && typeof $result.status.error === 'string'
  after:
    - name: Stop the bot
      do: Click Stop bot (if enabled).
      invoke: org.dxos.operation.thread.disconnectChannel { channel: Ref($channel.object) }
      expect: the banner reads Stopped or Disconnected
    - name: Delete the agent, channel and token
      do: Delete "QA: Interlocutor $runId", the "QA: discord $runId" channel and the "QA: bot $runId" token.
      invoke: org.dxos.operation.space.removeObjects { objects: [Obj($agent.agent), Obj($channel.object), Obj($token.object)] }
      expect: none of them remains; the agent's channel list and chats go with it (they are its children)
```

```mdl
test QA-2: Interview a person and confirm their goals
  desc: |
    The interview skill against a live model: the agent resolves the interviewee, records memories
    and proposes goals while the conversation runs, and confirming the read-back flips the goals to
    Confirmed in the Person's properties. The deterministic counterpart is the storybook play
    `stories-assistant` › Interview › TestInterviewScripted (scenario T-8), which scripts the model;
    it is a story, not a Playwright spec, so it is not listed under `automated:`.
  actors: human
  covers: [F-5.1, F-5.2, F-5.3, F-4.6]
  status: unverified         # pending — needs a live model key; never run against the app
  given:
    - an identity exists
    - space: a space is open and SPACE_READY
    - plugin-agent is enabled
    - a live model is available to the assistant (an Anthropic API key, or the EDGE AI service) — ABORT
      without one; a scripted model is the storybook's job, not this test's
    - no Person whose fullName contains "QA-$runId" exists
    - person: the Person the agent resolves in step 2 — resolved by the runner after that step as the
      one `org.dxos.type.person` whose fullName contains "QA-$runId"; the model creates it, so there is
      no invocation to `capture` it from
  before:
    - name: Create the interviewer
      do: Click + on the Agents section and rename the new agent "QA: Interviewer $runId".
      invoke: [op:createAgent] { name: "QA: Interviewer $runId" }
      capture: agent
      expect: the agent appears under Agents
  steps:
    - name: Open the agent's chat
      do: Click "QA: Interviewer $runId", then open its chat in the Assistant companion.
      invoke: org.dxos.operation.appToolkit.open { subject: ["root/$given.space.id/ai/org.dxos.type.agent/$agent.agent.objectId"] }
      expect: an empty chat with the agent is ready for input
    - name: Introduce yourself
      do: Send "Hi, I am Quinn QA-$runId."
      expect: |
        the agent states the purpose of the interview in a sentence and asks one open question; the
        trace shows a resolveEntity call
      note: NO `invoke` — the model chooses the tool calls. Human-only.
    - name: State two goals
      do: |
        Send "I want the QA demo working by Friday, and to hire one designer this year." Answer any
        follow-up question briefly until the agent reads the goals back and asks you to confirm them.
      expect: |
        each turn asks one question and reflects back what it heard; the read-back names both goals
      note: NO `invoke` — model-driven.
    - name: See the proposed goals and memories
      do: Open Quinn's Properties panel.
      invoke: [op:retrieveMemories] { subject: Ref($given.person) }
      capture: proposed
      expect: |
        the panel has a "Goals" region listing the two goals, each "Proposed", and a "Memories" region
        with at least one memory marked Stated
      assert: |
        return $result.goals.length >= 2
          && $result.goals.every((goal) => goal.status === 'proposed')
          && $result.memories.some((memory) => memory.origin === 'stated');
      note: Scoped to `$given.person`, whose name carries `$runId`, so goals left by an earlier run cannot count.
    - name: Confirm the goals
      do: Send "Yes, both are right."
      expect: the agent says it confirmed the goals and summarises what it recorded
      note: NO `invoke` — model-driven.
    - name: See the goals confirmed
      do: Look at Quinn's Properties panel again, without reopening it.
      invoke: [op:retrieveMemories] { subject: Ref($given.person) }
      capture: final
      expect: every goal in the Goals region now reads "Confirmed", and the same goals are listed
      assert: |
        const titles = (goals) => goals.map((goal) => goal.title).sort().join('\n');
        return $result.goals.length === $proposed.goals.length
          && titles($result.goals) === titles($proposed.goals)
          && $result.goals.every((goal) => goal.status === 'confirmed');
      note: The panel must update in place (F-5.3); a refresh only after reopening is a failure of this step.
  after:
    - name: Delete the interview's goals and memories
      do: Delete the goals and memories listed in Quinn's properties.
      invoke: org.dxos.operation.space.removeObjects { objects: [...$final.goals.map((row) => Obj(row.goal)), ...$final.memories.map((row) => Obj(row.memory))] }
      expect: Quinn's properties read "Nothing recorded yet."
      note: Superseded memories are not recalled; the runner also removes any Memory whose HasSubject targets `$given.person`.
    - name: Delete the person
      do: Delete Quinn.
      invoke: org.dxos.operation.space.removeObjects { objects: [Obj($given.person)] }
      expect: no Person whose fullName contains "QA-$runId" remains; the profile document goes with it (it is a child)
    - name: Delete the interviewer
      do: Right-click "QA: Interviewer $runId" and choose Delete agent.
      invoke: org.dxos.operation.space.removeObjects { objects: [Obj($agent.agent)] }
      expect: the agent and its chat leave the navtree
```

```mdl
test QA-3: Two people, one agent — a watch fires across private chats
  desc: |
    Two members, each on their own device and HALO identity, talk to the same agent in their own
    private chats. One asks to be kept posted on the other's work; when the other says what they are
    working on, the agent reads it into its brain, the watch wakes, and the update reaches the first
    member's chat. Facts live only in the agent's brain (BrainService), never in ECHO, so the Brain
    companion's Facts tab is the only place they show. Recorded side by side by autocue's `pair.mjs`
    (`autocue/two-people-one-agent.mjs`).
  actors: human
  covers: [F-1.1]
  status: unverified         # needs a live model and an EDGE that hosts the agent's chats and brain
  given:
    - two devices, each with its own identity: Alice (host) and Bob (guest), display names set
    - plugin-agent is enabled on both (it is tagged labs)
    - Composer points at an EDGE that runs agent chats (`Agent.chatLocation` is `edge` for an agent's chat)
      and serves a live model — ABORT without one
  steps:
    - name: Alice creates a space and an agent
      do: Alice creates a space "Launch room", then clicks + on the AI group's Agents section.
      invoke: [op:createAgent] { name: "" }
      capture: agent
      expect: the agent appears under Agents and its article opens Alice's private chat
    - name: Alice invites Bob; Bob joins
      do: Alice opens Members, creates a single-use invitation; Bob joins with the code and the auth code.
      expect: Bob's navtree shows "Launch room" with the same agent under Agents
    - name: Each opens a private chat with the agent
      do: Both click the agent under Agents.
      expect: |
        each sees an empty chat ready for input; neither sees the other's conversation (each chat is the
        member's own participant chat, hosted on EDGE)
      note: NO `invoke` — the article opens the viewer's chat through op OpenPrivateChat with their identity DID.
    - name: Alice asks to be kept posted on Bob
      do: Alice sends "Keep me posted on what Bob is working on."
      expect: the agent acknowledges; the Brain companion's Goals tab lists Alice's goal with a watch on Bob
      note: NO `invoke` — the model chooses watchFacts.
    - name: Bob says what he is working on
      do: Bob sends "I'm working on the indexer migration this week."
      expect: the agent replies to Bob
      note: NO `invoke` — model-driven; the end-request hook (runTriggers) reads the turn into the brain.
    - name: Alice receives the update
      do: Watch Alice's chat, without sending anything.
      expect: a message from the agent appears in Alice's chat telling her Bob is working on the indexer migration
      note: A watch whose update never reaches Alice's chat is a failure of this step, not of the model.
    - name: The facts are in the brain
      do: Open the agent's Brain companion and its Facts tab.
      expect: |
        the Facts tab lists a fact that Bob works on the indexer migration, attributed to Bob; no Memory
        or annotation object holds it in the space
```

```mdl
test QA-4: An agent invited into another space keeps one conversation and one brain
  desc: |
    The cross-space journey: an agent made in one space learns about something in conversation, is added to
    a second space from its Members panel the way a contact is, and there continues the same conversation and
    reads what it learned from its brain with a tool call. The autocue flow `autocue/agent-across-spaces.mjs`
    walks it and records the demo.
  actors: human
  covers: [F-1.4, F-6.1, F-6.4, F-6.5]
  status: passing             # 2026-10-13, recorded by autocue/agent-across-spaces.mjs against EDGE preview
  given:
    - an identity exists, with the display name "Mykola"
    - space: "My Space" is open and SPACE_READY, with no agent
    - other: a second space "Offsite team" exists, which Mykola owns
    - plugin-agent is enabled
    - a live model is available to the assistant (EDGE's AI service) — ABORT without one
  steps:
    - name: Create the agent
      do: In My Space click + ("Add to space"), pick Agent, name it "Kai" and click Create.
      invoke: [op:createAgent] { name: "Kai" }
      capture: agent
      expect: Kai is listed under Assistant › Agents and its empty chat opens
    - name: Tell Kai about the offsite
      do: Send "Hi Kai, I'm Mykola. Our team offsite is in Lisbon on November 14, for 12 people, and Dima is booking the venue."
      expect: Kai acknowledges the place, the date and the headcount in a short reply
      note: NO `invoke` — the model replies; the turn's facts are read into Kai's brain after it.
    - name: Add the budget
      do: Send "The budget is 8,000 euros, and flights are still open."
      expect: Kai acknowledges the budget
      note: NO `invoke` — model-driven.
    - name: Add Kai to the other space from its Members panel
      do: In Offsite team open Settings › Members; under "Add from contacts" pick Kai and click Add.
      invoke: [op:inviteAgent] { agent: Ref($agent.agent) }   # run in $given.other
      capture: presence
      expect: Offsite team lists Kai under Assistant › Agents, and the picker no longer offers Kai
      assert: return $result.created === true
    - name: Open Kai in the other space
      do: Click Kai under Offsite team's Agents.
      expect: the same conversation as in My Space opens — Mykola's two messages and Kai's replies; there is one conversation, not one per space
    - name: Ask Kai to check its memory
      do: Send "Check your memory: what do we have so far for the offsite?"
      expect: |
        Kai calls Recall (shown as a tool row) and answers from its brain — Lisbon, November 14, 12 people,
        Dima on the venue, the 8,000 euro budget, flights open
      note: NO `invoke` — model-driven; the facts come from Kai's brain through op:retrieveMemories.
    - name: Read Kai's brain from the other space
      do: Open Kai's Brain companion and its Facts tab.
      expect: the facts Kai read from the My Space conversation are listed, attributed to Mykola
  after:
    - name: Delete Kai in both spaces
      do: Delete Kai in Offsite team, then in My Space.
      invoke: org.dxos.operation.space.removeObjects { objects: [Obj($presence.agent)] }   # then [Obj($agent.agent)] in $given.space
      expect: neither space lists Kai
```

```mdl
test QA-5: Two admins — Bob, made Kai's admin by Alice, adds Kai to his own space, where it recalls what Alice told it
  desc: |
    Admins are an agent's space's members. Alice creates an agent, which mints its identity and its own space on
    EDGE; she adds Bob to that space the way she would add him to any space, and both find the agent in Profile ›
    Agents. Alice then tells the agent about the team offsite, and Bob, who cannot reach Alice's space, adds the agent
    to his own space from its Members panel and asks it what is planned: it answers from its brain. The autocue flow
    `autocue/agent-admins.mjs` walks it side by side, one browser per person.
  actors: human
  covers: [F-6.1, F-6.5, F-6.7, F-7.1, F-7.2, F-7.4, F-7.5, F-7.6, F-7.7]
  status: passing             # 2026-10-13, recorded by autocue/agent-admins.mjs against a local EDGE (dxos/edge#1293)
  given:
    - alice: an identity with the display name "Alice", on one device
    - bob: an identity with the display name "Bob", on another device
    - other: Bob's own space "Offsite team"
    - plugin-agent is enabled for both
    - EDGE with brain identities (`POST /compute/brain`) is reachable — ABORT without it
    - a live model is available to the assistant (EDGE's AI service) — ABORT without one
  steps:
    - name: Alice creates an agent named Kai
      do: In Alice's space click + ("Add to space"), pick Agent, name it "Kai" and click Create.
      invoke: [op:createAgent] { name: "Kai" }
      capture: agent
      expect: Kai's chat opens at once, and a space named Kai appears in Alice's space list once EDGE has minted it
      note: Alice's space is her personal one, which is locked; its owner still admits Kai as a member (F-7.6).
    - name: Alice finds Kai in Profile › Agents
      do: Open Alice's profile and its Agents panel, listed after Devices.
      expect: Kai is listed with "Admins: Alice (you)"
    - name: Alice invites Bob into Kai's space
      do: Click Kai's space chip on its row; in Kai's space open Settings › Members and invite one person.
      expect: an invitation code is ready to share
    - name: Bob joins Kai's space
      do: In Bob's window click + ("Add space"), choose Join, enter the invitation code and then the PIN.
      expect: Bob's space list shows Kai
    - name: Bob finds Kai in Profile › Agents
      do: Open Bob's profile and its Agents panel.
      expect: Kai is listed with "Admins: Alice, Bob (you)"
    - name: Alice sees Bob among Kai's admins
      do: Open Alice's Agents panel again.
      expect: Kai is listed with "Admins: Alice (you), Bob"
    - name: Alice tells Kai about the team offsite
      do: In Alice's space open Kai and send "Hi Kai, I'm Alice. Our team offsite is in Lisbon on November 14, for 12 people, and Dima is booking the venue."
      expect: Kai acknowledges the place, the date and the headcount in a short reply
      note: NO `invoke` — the model replies on EDGE; the turn's facts are read into Kai's brain after it.
    - name: Alice adds the budget
      do: Send "The budget is 8,000 euros, and flights are still open."
      expect: Kai acknowledges the budget
      note: NO `invoke` — model-driven.
    - name: Bob adds Kai to his own space from its Members panel
      do: In Bob's window open Offsite team, then Settings › Members; under "Add from contacts" pick Kai and click Add.
      invoke: [op:inviteAgent] { agent: Ref($agent.agent) }   # run in $given.other, as Bob
      expect: the picker lists Alice and Kai, each once; after Add, Offsite team lists Kai under Agents and among its members
      note: Bob is not a member of Alice's space, where Kai lives; the picker finds Kai through Kai's own space, which records its home (F-7.5), and EDGE accepts the space because Bob is Kai's admin (F-6.7).
    - name: Bob opens Kai in Offsite team
      do: Click Kai under Offsite team's Agents.
      expect: Bob's own chat with Kai opens, empty; Alice's conversation stays in her space
    - name: Bob asks Kai about the offsite
      do: Send "Check your memory: what do we have so far for the offsite?"
      expect: |
        Kai calls Recall and answers from its brain with what Alice told it, naming her: at least Lisbon,
        November 14, 12 people and the 8,000 euro budget
      note: NO `invoke` — model-driven; these words were never said in Offsite team. On 2026-10-13 Kai answered "So far for the offsite: Lisbon, November 14, for 12 people, with a budget of €8,000 — all per Alice."
  after:
    - name: Remove Kai from Offsite team and Bob from Kai's space
      do: In Offsite team delete Kai; in Kai's space open Settings › Members and remove Bob.
      expect: Offsite team no longer lists Kai, and Bob's Agents panel no longer lists Kai
```

---

## Appendix: Extension Definitions

Extension block types used in this document are defined below using the core `ext` primitive. `op`,
`test` and `step` are defined in `lang/qa.mdl`, `node` and `surface` in `lang/app.mdl`, and `skill`
in `plugin-crm/PLUGIN.mdl`.

```mdl
ext type
  uri: org.dxos.mdl.type@1.0
  desc: A named data structure with typed fields and optional literals.
  fields:
    desc?: Prose
    fields?: FieldMap        # name[?]: TypeExpr  (# inline comment)
    literals?: UnionList     # a | b | c
    extends?: TypeRef[]
```

```mdl
ext feat
  uri: org.dxos.mdl.feat@1.0
  desc: A named feature grouping one or more requirements.
  fields:
    desc?: Prose
    req: RequirementList
  nesting: self              # feat blocks may contain feat blocks
```

```mdl
ext scenario
  uri: org.dxos.mdl.scenario@1.0
  desc: An acceptance scenario expressed as given / when / then steps.
  fields:
    given?: Step | Step[]
    when?: Step | Step[]
    then: Step | Step[]
    tags?: TagList
```

```mdl
ext component
  uri: org.dxos.mdl.component@1.0
  desc: A UI component with props, internal state, slots, actions, and events.
  fields:
    desc?: Prose
    props?: FieldMap            # external inputs (immutable inside component)
    state?: FieldMap            # internal reactive state
    slots?: FieldMap            # named ReactNode injection points
    actions?: ActionMap         # methods the component exposes or handles
    emits?: EventMap            # events the component raises to its parent
    layout?: CodeBlock          # ASCII sketch of visual structure (non-normative)
```
